WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.467 --> 00:00:05.071
Computing on the cloud essentially means that your data is on
someone else's computer.

00:00:05.071 --> 00:00:09.876
And with the global average cost of a data breach
being 4.45 million dollars,

00:00:09.876 --> 00:00:15.248
or a violation of the EU's General Data Protection
Regulation (GDPR) being a hefty fine,

00:00:15.615 --> 00:00:19.853
it's easy to admit that remaining security compliant
with your data is extremely important.

00:00:20.153 --> 00:00:23.390
That's where Data Security Posture Management (DSPM) comes into play.

00:00:23.957 --> 00:00:26.860
DSPM solutions puts you at the helm of your cloud

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:31.264
data inventory, interactions and access,
then gives you a platform to help

00:00:31.531 --> 00:00:36.069
mitigate risks, find and remediate vulnerabilities, and ensure
a compliant security posture.

00:00:36.436 --> 00:00:41.107
It does this with three things in mind: visibility,
movement and protection.

00:00:41.508 --> 00:00:45.145
And to explain these things,
I'll use this example topology diagram.

00:00:45.678 --> 00:00:47.814
First problem, where is our data stored?

00:00:48.281 --> 00:00:50.550
It's not just in the databases.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:53.920
Sensitive information
gets passed to all components of your topology.

00:00:54.387 --> 00:00:57.190
What if sensitive information is in an instant message

00:00:57.524 --> 00:01:00.860
or Excel spreadsheet or PDF document?

00:01:01.361 --> 00:01:05.031
What if someone creates their own version of the database
for development purposes?

00:01:05.365 --> 00:01:09.102
Then most likely this database is not going to have
the same controls as the original.

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:14.674
For that, you need visibility. And DSPM solutions
help to identify all locations where data resides,

00:01:15.008 --> 00:01:19.245
even shadow data
that's not specifically identified in your official inventory.

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:22.482
Next problem, where does the data go?

00:01:22.849 --> 00:01:27.654
Well, to know where the data goes,
you need to know its access points.

00:01:28.388 --> 00:01:33.059
Sure, we have these connections in our topology diagram,
but what about all of its users?

00:01:33.293 --> 00:01:34.861
Who has access to what?

00:01:34.861 --> 00:01:38.932
What kind of third parties do you interact with
and what access do they have?

00:01:39.365 --> 00:01:42.602
For that,
you need to know the movement. And DSPM solutions help

00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:45.271
to analyze both potential and actual flows of data.

00:01:45.839 --> 00:01:50.610
Last problem, what kind of risk do we face by having this data
and all of these different components?

00:01:50.977 --> 00:01:53.346
Well, all systems have vulnerabilities.

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:58.284
So which components are the most vulnerable?

00:01:58.284 --> 00:02:03.123
Are the third parties you interact with responsible
enough for the data they have access to?

00:02:03.389 --> 00:02:07.427
If a data leak did happen, do you have the safeguards in place
to mitigate the total effect?

00:02:07.727 --> 00:02:10.029
For that, you need data protection.

00:02:10.029 --> 00:02:13.299
And DSPM solutions help to uncover these vulnerabilities

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:17.337
in your data, interactions and access and compliance controls,

00:02:17.704 --> 00:02:21.474
and then gives you a risk based prioritization

00:02:21.474 --> 00:02:25.645
of these vulnerabilities and remediation recommendations.

00:02:26.179 --> 00:02:30.483
Okay, so once we have these three things in place,
then we can start getting some payoffs.

00:02:30.717 --> 00:02:33.153
First, reducing exposed data.

00:02:33.553 --> 00:02:36.923
For this,
you can make sure that your data is securely shared with only

00:02:36.923 --> 00:02:40.460
its intended recipients
and inaccessible to unauthorized parties.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:44.097
Then you can remove any sensitive information from your cloud

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:46.699
operations or various SaaS applications.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:50.069
Next up, you can prevent data leakage.

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:54.040
And you do this by safeguarding the data
and making sure it doesn't go into the wrong hands.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:59.979
You can monitor your flows and transactions between different
cloud environments and your various SaaS applications.

00:03:00.013 --> 00:03:04.250
Along with the previous two benefits,
you can better mitigate third party exposure

00:03:04.551 --> 00:03:09.789
by analyzing all of your third party vendors
and what kind of data they have access to.

00:03:10.056 --> 00:03:15.128
Then determining whether or not they have
the necessary certifications to handle such information.

00:03:15.461 --> 00:03:19.265
And once you have that information,
you can decide whether or not you're going to maintain

00:03:19.265 --> 00:03:23.970
or exclude their access to that data and streamline
this risk assessment of your third party vendors.

00:03:24.237 --> 00:03:28.274
Last but certainly not least,
you can monitor data transactions.

00:03:28.274 --> 00:03:32.645
And this allows you to oversee data transactions
between different countries and regions

00:03:32.879 --> 00:03:35.848
that may have those data retention regulations in place.

00:03:36.182 --> 00:03:38.651
This way allows you to remain security compliant.

00:03:39.485 --> 00:03:44.557
So, if you're going to take away anything from this video,
know that data security is very important.

00:03:44.557 --> 00:03:48.895
And if it's not obvious by now,
there's a lot of issues that go along with it.

00:03:49.028 --> 00:03:53.032
So to help you out,
check out Data Security Posture Management solutions.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:54.801
Thanks for watching.

00:03:54.801 --> 00:03:59.239
Before you leave, please remember to hit like and subscribe.

